          <h2>Lesson 1: Paper Recall on the Pre-Homeostasis Topics</h2>

          <p class="section-intro">
            Today we go off-screen. Laptops shut, paper out. The activity is a free-recall
            sweep across everything we covered in Weeks 3 to 6 &mdash; the animal-responses
            content that feeds AS 91603. The point is not just to revise. It's for you to
            <em>feel</em> why paper-based recall is the most efficient revision tool you
            have, before I ask you to use it again on your own.
          </p>


          <h3>What You Need</h3>

          <ul>
            <li>A blank piece of paper (or a fresh page in your exercise book).</li>
            <li>A pen.</li>
            <li>A green pen or highlighter for the marking pass at the end.</li>
            <li>That's it. No laptop. No notes. No textbook. No phone.</li>
          </ul>


          <h3>The Activity</h3>

          <p>
            Four prompts. <strong>Five to seven minutes per prompt.</strong> When the timer
            starts, you write everything you can remember about that topic. Bullet points,
            sentences, sketches, arrows, any format that helps you. After each prompt, pens
            down, and we share out two or three answers as a class before moving on. Notes
            stay closed between prompts &mdash; the gaps are doing the work.
          </p>

          <ol class="step-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Simple animal responses (Wk 3).</strong> What counts as a stimulus?
              What counts as a response? What are the categories of simple response we
              looked at? Examples in named species. How does this differ from a learned
              response?
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Taxes, kineses and pheromones (Wk 4).</strong> All the types of taxes
              you can name (positive / negative; phototaxis, chemotaxis, geotaxis, and the
              rest). What is a kinesis and how does it differ from a taxis? What do
              pheromones do? Examples in named species.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The waggle dance and homing (Wk 5).</strong> What information does the
              waggle dance encode? How is the dance decoded by other bees? How do homing
              animals navigate &mdash; sun compass, magnetic, landmarks, olfactory? Pick a
              named species for each.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Migration (Wks 5&ndash;6).</strong> What triggers migration
              physiologically &mdash; photoperiod, hormones, fat loading? How does the body
              prepare? What is the evolutionary advantage? What human impacts are
              disrupting migration today?
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h3>The Marking Pass (Last 10 Minutes)</h3>

          <p>
            When the four prompts are done, <em>now</em> open your notes. Mark your own
            paper in green pen.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Underline</strong> things you got right.</li>
            <li><strong>Circle</strong> things you got wrong.</li>
            <li><strong>Star</strong> things you missed entirely.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Hand the marked paper in at the end of the lesson, or photograph it for your
            study folder. The starred and circled items are your revision priority list for
            the rest of Term 2. The underlined items are what you actually own.
          </p>

          <div class="connection-box">
            <h4>The Honest Bit</h4>
            <p>
              You will write less than you'd expect. That's normal. The gap between what
              you think you know and what you can pull out cold is the most useful
              information you'll get this week. It is far more useful than re-reading
              everything and feeling fine.
            </p>
          </div>

        </section>


